<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where Is Your Book? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where Is Your Book?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Identify primary and secondary colors. interactive: Respond to classroom commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify colors by naming and pointing in response to classroom commands (e.g., 'Point to the red chair.' 'What color is this?'). Can respond to classroom commands with gestures or one-word answers (e.g., 'Show me the yellow pencil, please.' and the students hold up a yellow pencil). Can answer simple questions about location, using prepositions (e.g., 'Is the book on the table?').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Color and Object Hunt" activity. The teacher says, "Point to something red!" or "Show me a blue pencil, please." Students point to objects in the classroom or hold up colored pencils. The teacher encourages students to say the color and object if they can (e.g., "Red chair!"). This activates prior knowledge of colors and classroom items from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a book and a desk. "Look! Here is a book. Here is a desk." The teacher places the book on the desk, saying clearly, "The book is ON the desk." (stressing 'on'). Then places it under the desk: "The book is UNDER the desk." The teacher models pronunciation of /w/ in 'where' by clearly articulating "Where is the book?" and /b/ in 'book' by repeating "book" slowly and distinctly. The teacher demonstrates polite requests: "Show me the yellow pencil, please." and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses realia and gestures.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher places a book on the table: "Is the book IN the table? (No.) Is the book ON the table? (Yes.)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher places a pencil under a chair: "Where is the pencil? Is it blue or red?" (A pencil. Blue!) "Is it under the chair?" (Yes!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short animated picture story or a sequence of large flashcards depicting a child looking for their 'book' (or other classroom items like a 'pencil', 'marker', 'bag') in different locations (on, in, under the desk, chair, or box). The story features simple dialogues like: "Oh no! Where is my book?" "Is it on the chair?" "No, it's not on the chair." "Is it under the desk?" "Yes! It's under the desk!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions. Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions. Sociolinguistic: Polite request: Asking politely about the location of items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After showing the story, the teacher asks, "What is the child looking for?" (Book!) "Does the child find the book?" (Yes!).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> The teacher shows a picture from the story (e.g., book on the desk) and says, "The book is under the chair. True or False?" (False!). Students give a thumbs up for true, thumbs down for false.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher asks, "Point to where the book is on the table." Students point to the correct image on the flashcard or screen.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: productive (identifying colors, responding to commands), interactive (responding to questions with prepositions). Grammar: Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to), WH-Questions for Location ('Where is...?'). Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, marker, colors (yellow, red, blue, orange, green, purple). Pronunciation: /w/ in 'where', /b/ in 'book'. Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing letter sounds (K, L, M, N, O) through object names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Teacher Says, You Find!" (Choral/Individual Response):</b> The teacher places various colored objects (book, pencil, marker, bag) in different known locations (on the desk, under the chair, in the box). The teacher asks, "Where is the red book?" Students respond chorally "On the desk!" The teacher then points to another item: "What color is this pencil? Where is it?" (Yellow! Under the chair!). Teacher focuses on clear pronunciation of /w/ and /b/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Is It...?" Game (Yes/No & Preposition Practice):</b> The teacher hides an object (e.g., a green marker) in a classroom location. Students take turns asking "Is the green marker on the table?" The teacher responds "No, it isn't." The next student asks "Is the green marker under the chair?" If correct, the teacher says "Yes, it is! It's under the chair!" and shows the item. Students practice one-word or short sentence answers and asking questions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Ask and Show, Please" (Polite Requests):</b> Students work in pairs with a small collection of classroom objects (e.g., book, pencil, marker, ruler) and a designated 'desk' and 'chair' area on their own desk. One student says, "Show me the blue book, please." The other student finds the blue book and places it in a location, then asks, "Where is the blue book?" The first student responds with a preposition (e.g., "It's on the chair."). They swap roles. Teacher circulates, prompts for politeness ("please"), and corrects pronunciation.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Detective" game in small groups of 3-4 students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a small box, a few colored classroom objects (e.g., red book, yellow pencil, blue marker, green bag), and a "mystery card" (a picture depicting one of their objects in a specific location, e.g., 'the red book under the desk').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One "Detective" student in each group closes their eyes. Another student (the "Hider") hides the object shown on the mystery card in their group's designated area (using a mini-desk, chair, box, etc.).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Detective" opens their eyes and asks, "Where is the red book?" The "Hider" and other group members respond with clues using prepositions and colors (e.g., "Is it on the chair?" "No, it's not on the chair." "Is it under the desk?" "Yes, it's under the desk!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once the object is found, students rotate roles. The teacher circulates, facilitating interaction, prompting for full sentence answers (even if simple: "It is under the desk."), and providing support with vocabulary and pronunciation (especially /w/ and /b/).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Detective" game and a quick individual "Show and Tell" at the end.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, students respond to the teacher's "Where is...?" questions by pointing and giving a one-word or short phrase answer (e.g., "on," "under," "on the table"). They also follow one-step commands (e.g., "Put the yellow book on your desk, please.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (colors, objects, prepositions, /w/, /b/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (colors, book, pencil, desk, chair, in, on, under) correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Where is...?" questions with a relevant preposition (e.g., "under," "on").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows classroom commands involving location (e.g., "Put the book on the table.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates collaboratively in group tasks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "Happy Face" or "Sad Face" card in response to:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell where the book is? (e.g., 'on', 'under')?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I follow 'Put the book on the desk'?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy today? Saying 'on' or 'under'?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I ask 'Where is the blue book?' to my friend?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Classroom Detective" game worked well for student engagement and speaking practice, particularly with the prepositions 'in', 'on', and 'under'. Some students struggled with 'next to' and forming full questions ("Where is...?"), often just pointing. More explicit modeling and choral repetition of "Where is the...?" and sentence frames for responses (e.g., "It is...") will be needed. Incorporating more TPR for 'behind' and 'in front of' might also help. Phonemic awareness practice for K, L, M, N, O was integrated through object names, but could be explicitly linked to flashcards in the next lesson's warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build upon the identification of objects and their locations by having students draw their own classroom scene and label items, or write simple sentences about where objects are, reinforcing vocabulary and prepositions in a written context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>